{{ template "chart.description" . }}

This chart installs the five cluster-wide COSI APIs: Bucket, BucketClaim,
BucketClass, BucketAccess, and BucketAccessClass. It installs no controller,
driver, credentials, or custom resource instances. Install it once per cluster,
independently of driver releases.

## Compatibility

`appVersion` pins the upstream source used by `just generate-crds`. This chart
currently contains COSI **v0.2.2 / v1alpha1** definitions, matching this branch's
controller overlay and default sidecar. Chart versions are independent of driver
release versions. Bump this chart's `version` whenever changing its packaged resources.

Do not use these definitions with v1alpha2 components. Replacing v1alpha1 schemas
with v1alpha2 is not an in-place migration: existing resources and stored API
versions require an explicit migration plan. A Helm upgrade does not provide API
conversion or migrate stored objects.

## Install

From a checkout containing this chart:

```sh
helm upgrade --install cosi-crds ./helm/cosi-crds \
  --namespace container-object-storage-system --create-namespace --wait
kubectl wait --for=condition=Established --timeout=60s \
  crd/buckets.objectstorage.k8s.io \
  crd/bucketclaims.objectstorage.k8s.io \
  crd/bucketclasses.objectstorage.k8s.io \
  crd/bucketaccesses.objectstorage.k8s.io \
  crd/bucketaccessclasses.objectstorage.k8s.io

# Install the upstream controller without applying a second copy of its CRDs.
just deploy-controller
```

Then install the matching Linode driver chart as described in the root README.
This chart is not a dependency of the driver chart. After publication, it can
also be installed as `linode-cosi-driver/cosi-crds` from the Helm repository.

## Upgrades and retention

The definitions are rendered through `templates/`, rather than Helm's
install-only `crds/` directory. `helm upgrade cosi-crds ./helm/cosi-crds` therefore
applies schema changes. Review the rendered diff and compatibility with existing
objects before upgrading, and update the CRDs before deploying components that
require the new schema. Workload rollback does not roll back this separate
release, and schema rollback may be incompatible with stored resources.

Each CRD has `helm.sh/resource-policy: keep`. Helm retains the CRDs and their
instances when this release is uninstalled or a CRD is removed from its rendered
manifest. Uninstall does not delete the release namespace. Explicit CRD deletion
with Kubernetes tools still deletes its custom resource instances; the annotation
is a Helm policy, not an API-server protection mechanism.

After uninstall, retained CRDs are no longer managed by an active Helm release.
Reinstall using the same release name and namespace; different ownership metadata
requires a deliberate handoff. Install only one CRD release per cluster.

## Existing installations

Helm will reject existing CRDs owned by another release or lacking its ownership
metadata. Keep externally managed CRDs under their current management, or plan an
ownership handoff before installing this chart. Do not delete CRDs to resolve an
ownership conflict, and do not use forced resource replacement for schema upgrades.

The existing Tilt workflow continues to apply the upstream kustomization directly;
it does not use this chart's Helm release lifecycle. Avoid reconciling the same
CRDs through both Tilt/kustomize and this Helm release.

For controller removal in this Helm-managed setup, use `just undeploy-controller`;
it leaves the CRDs and release namespace intact. The legacy `just undeploy-deps`
command explicitly deletes upstream CRDs and is not an uninstall path for this setup.

## Development and validation

```sh
just generate-crds       # regenerate from the source pinned in Chart.yaml
just generate-docs       # regenerate this README
just test-crds-chart     # disposable KIND cluster; no Linode credentials needed
```

The lifecycle test checks fresh installation, a compatible schema upgrade,
retention when a CRD is omitted by an upgrade, retention of all five CRDs and
their instances on uninstall, and reinstallation.
The upgrade fixture adds an optional field to a temporary chart copy; generated
upstream definitions in the repository are not modified by the test.

The release workflow publishes this chart under its own chart version and a
`cosi-crds-<version>` GitHub release name. Driver chart releases retain their
existing `helm-v<version>` naming.
